Dependencies make names visible. Every source dependency resolves from the binding file's owning module root, not the file's directory.

A path has three jobs:

- `@card render_card` at the start of a statement is a dependency clause. It binds `render_card`.
- `@settings site_name` inside an expression reads one value. It binds nothing.
- `@docs/intro.mtf` is a file value. It gives a `String`.

### A module-root-relative dependency

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @card render_card
]

`@card` resolves to `card.moth` under the binding file's owning module root, regardless of how deep the binding file is inside the module.

### A support package dependency

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @markdown render
]

`@markdown` resolves to a scoped `@package.moth` support package visible in the binding module's lexical scope.

### A qualified read

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    title = @settings site_name
]

`@settings` names a module or helper and `site_name` names one declaration. The path and the symbol stay on one line. The read creates no local `site_name`.

### A file value

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    intro = @docs/intro.mtf
]

The extension makes this a file value, not a dependency. A `.moth` path has no file value. Depend on its declarations instead.

Root files such as `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` and `@entry.moth` are never dependency targets. Depend on the module folder.

`export:` remains current. A later `$export` migration will replace it only in `@mod.moth` and `@package.moth`. It does not change module-root-relative resolution.
